WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.629
It appears that Marvel and DC fans have slightly
different preferences when it comes to how

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:07.299
they get their respective fixes.

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:13.210
A new survey conducted by streaming and cord-cutting
news site Flixed examining the attitudes of

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.480
Americans toward streaming vs. movie theater
viewing revealed that Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.490
aficionados are more likely than DC die-hards
to catch the latest installment in the theater.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.820
The difference was relatively slight, but
with millions upon millions of dollars at

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.860
play each time a superhero tentpole picture
hits screens, it still feels pretty significant.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.640
"You're not the only one cursed with knowledge."

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.629
"My only curse it you!"

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:46.629
Survey respondents were asked if they would
consider catching the next installment of

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:51.579
four popular franchises at home rather than
in the theater, with those uninterested in

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:54.780
any given franchise being excluded from the
results.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.950
When it came to Mighty Marvel vs. the Distinguished
Competition, 34 percent insisted that they

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:06.140
would rather see the next MCU flick in the
theater, as compared to 27 percent for the

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.840
DC movie universe.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.800
Again, those seven percentage points make
a pretty big difference when you're talking

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.590
about millions of moviegoers, and those numbers
are suggestive of a couple different things.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:22.160
First, it's pretty obvious that Marvel Studios
has utterly captured the hearts of fans, considering

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.530
that a plurality of moviegoers of all ages
would be willing to pay a premium to watch

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.570
first-run films in the comfort of their homes.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.530
The fact that the next MCU movie, whatever
it might be, would entice fully a third of

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.902
those who want to see it to head out to the
theater even if they had the option to watch

00:01:38.902 --> 00:01:40.840
at home is pretty impressive.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.470
Second, the DC cinematic universe may have
suffered a bit from the comparatively poor

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.670
quality of its early offerings, but that doesn't
mean that fans still aren't on board with

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.890
what Warner Bros. and DC Films are cooking
up next.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.630
With over a quarter of them preferring the
theater experience over watching at home for

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.170
the next installment, it's clear that there's
still a great deal of goodwill toward the

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:07.270
franchise; recent hits such as Aquaman, Shazam!,
and Joker have probably helped quite a bit

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.789
in this regard.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:15.400
As for the other two franchises, 34% of interested
moviegoers the same number scored by the MCU

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.140
asserted that the next entry in the Disney-owned
Star Wars franchise would be a must-watch

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.460
in the theater.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.450
The final franchise in question: Warner Bros.-owned
Harry Potter, which could draw 25% of its

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.150
fans back to the theater with another installment.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.680
These were just a few of the interesting tidbits
of information yielded by the study, which

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.650
took a broad view of the opinions of a wide
cross-section of Americans regarding the convenience

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.320
of streaming vs. the experience of theater
viewing.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.130
Respondents were lumped into the categories
of "Millennial," "Gen X," and "Baby Boomer"

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:50.320
and if you think you've got each age range
pegged, you're likely to be shocked at some

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.510
of the results.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.350
For example, when it came to the simple question
of whether respondents would prefer to see

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.830
new movies in general at home or in theaters,
you might think that Millennials would be

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.560
all about the streaming as compared to the
other age groups but if you did think that,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.370
you would apparently be wrong.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:12.130
By far, Gen X respondents came down most heavily
on the side of home viewing, with a whopping

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.910
73% saying that they would prefer to stream
new releases.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.230
As for the responses of Millennials and Boomers
to the question, they were not that different.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:25.390
61% of the former preferred home viewing as
compared to 63% of the latter, and factoring

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:30.480
in neutral responses, the percentage of respondents
from each age group that straight-up preferred

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.800
theater viewing was the same 31%.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.450
Regardless of age group, the survey's responses
indicated that the forthcoming, so-called

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.790
"streaming wars" are no joke.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:45.340
Fully two-thirds of respondents also asserted
that as streaming services proliferate in

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:50.000
earnest over the next three years, they will
definitely see fewer movies in theaters even

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.200
though slightly over half of those responding
agreed that movies produced for in-theater

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.580
viewing (as opposed to streaming originals)
were generally more entertaining.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.230
As to the franchise-specific question, though,
one could argue that Disney and Warner Bros.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.590
should take very different things away from
the responses.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.310
Disney would be well-served to keep doing
what it's been doing, particularly as that

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:14.730
pertains to the MCU; Warner Bros., on the
other hand, could stand to double down on

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.780
its recalibration efforts of the last couple
years.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.460
One final note: it should be very interesting
to see, with Marvel Studios planning to extend

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:28.260
the MCU's tendrils to the small screen via
soon-to-debut streamer Disney+, whether fans

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.240
will be just as psyched to catch the next
chapter in theaters in, say, two or three

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.240
years.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.870
Marvel has consistently gotten butts in theater
seats for over a decade, and the studio scored

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:43.650
the highest-grossing film of all time in Avengers:
Endgame ... but the times are a-changin'.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.940
"I see this as an absolute win."

